			<div class="content" id="post-content-4831398">
									

<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">Here, have a Placeholder. I'll crit this within 24 hours.</span></p>
<p>Did I just disappear from existence for 3 days? Yeah, I think I just did.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Here,&nbsp;have&nbsp;some&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[-]&nbsp;OK,&nbsp;now&nbsp;give&nbsp;me&nbsp;back&nbsp;my&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>So, right of the bat, I have a nitpick to always put a line (<span style="text-decoration: line-through;">-</span>-) between the ACS and the rest of the article. This is a personal thing, so you're not obligated to do this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX and all instances of SCP-XXXX-1 [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Are all of them in the same containment chamber? Please specify this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1 instances are to be kept on a metal frame in the middle of this container so that they do not have direct contact with any side of the box.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A little rewording here. Maybe try <em>A metal frame is to be located in the middle of the container, in order to prevent SCP-XXXX-1 instances from touching the sides.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Any room located directly above, below, or adjacent to SCP-XXXX's location are not to be used as dormitories or for sleep studies except as part of an approved experiment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>More rewording here: <em>Any rooms located directly above, below, or adjacent to SCP-XXXX's containment chamber are not to be used to sleep, unless approved for testing purposes.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Following incident SCP-XXXX-I2 (see incident report below), personnel should notice and report any unusual topography in the hallways and rooms surrounding SCP-XXXX's containment cell or lab rooms to Dr. Theresa Mathers or Site-11 Containment officials.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;SCP-XXXX-I2&quot; may confuse the reader to think there are more anomalies. I would suggest rewording this part to: <em>Following Incident XXXX.I2 (see below), should personnel notice any unusual topography in the hallways and rooms surrounding SCP-XXXX's containment cell, they are to report it to either Dr. Theresa Mather or Site-11 Containment Department.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the event of a containment breach a human corpse may be used [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you forgot a comma between &quot;breach&quot; and &quot;a human&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This section is pending revision following incident SCP-XXXX-I2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would suggest to put a RAISA notice at the top of the article, informing that some parts of the article are still pending revision and that the updated version will be uploaded soon or something. You can find the RAISA code <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-style-resource#toc4">here</a>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">a standard spring based twin sized mattress</span>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why did you crossed this out? You already stated that the information was outdated.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[&#8230;] it is self-healing [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would suggest to explain this part. How does it heals? At what rate?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX contains twenty-four localities of increased firmness corresponding to the number of vertebrae in the human cervical, thoracic, and lumbar spine.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Couldn't this be a simple coincidence? Why would the researchers connect the number of increased firmness to the number of vertebrae in the human body? Maybe you could say they coincide with the location of the vertebrae when a person is lying on it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Persons who lie on SCP-XXXX, hereafter called Subjects, [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They're always called subjects. Just call them subjects every time you're referring to them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[&#8230;] report feeling these &quot;bumps&quot; move underneath them during the night.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe try: <em>report feeling these protuberances to move underneath them while laying on the object at night time.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subjects also report hearing threatening whispers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe you could give an example of these whispers?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Communication with the entity [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What entity? So far the object is just a mattress, and the &quot;whispers&quot; may just be another anomalous effect. I think that you could state the entity's motives in the example whispers.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subjects who are killed by SCP-XXXX [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You never said the object could kill people. I think you could add something along the lines of &quot;every subject that sleeps on SCP-XXXX during [timehere] have their vertebrae forcibly removed from their back, beng killed almost instantly.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Nevertheless following such an attack the surface of the and with the same length as the Subject's spinal column.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you ate some words in this sentence. Also, don't capitalize &quot;subject&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Experimentation with SCP-XXXX has shown that only human Subjects are attacked, with both living and dead Subjects experiencing the same outcome.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Some rewording here: <em>Experimentation with SCP-XXXX has shown that only human beings, dead or alive, are affected, both experiencing the same outcome.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>High speed photography of attacks [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The effect shouldn't be called &quot;attack&quot;. Since the Description is outdated, the Foundation shouldn't know that there is an entity living within the mattress. Either that, or you should specify that there's something living in it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Because of the arrangement of wounds, it is theorized that the SCP may manifest seven invisible digits with sharp claws during attacks.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>First, don't call the anomaly &quot;the SCP&quot;, just call it SCP-XXXX. Second, I think that this sentence is too expository. You could change it to: <em>Because of the wounds' arrangement, it appears that SCP-XXXX manifests some kind of sharp claws during the attack.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>04/27/20██</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Don't redact information unless you want to actually hide important information from the reader. The blackboxes draw attention and interrupt the read.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subject 0 puts SCP-XXXX up on ███████.com as a freecycled mattress.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe try: <em>SCP-XXXX is offered on an online site to sell freecycle objects.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Subject 0 is contacted by the mother of Subject 1, who agrees to pick up the mattress the next day</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe try: <em>The seller is contacted by another person who agrees to pick up the mattress the next day.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX is acquired by Subject 1's mother. She sets it up in a small bedroom. After initial excitement, Subject 1 reports being afraid to sleep in the room. She sleeps in her mother's bed instead</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe try: <em>SCP-XXXX is acquired by a single mother, who sets it up in her child's small bedroom. After her initial excitement, the child reports being afraid to sleep in her room. She sleeps with her mother instead.</em></p>
<p>Also, yeah, I think you should replace the &quot;Subject 1&quot; stuff with their proper pronouns,to make it feel more natural. (This is just me being too lazy to reword the rest of the timeline.)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[&#8230;] which piques the interest of an ex-Foundation investigator on the team</p>
</blockquote>
<p>When someone gets fired or retired from the Foundation, they get amnesticized. I think the Foundation could just get alerted by a normal mole within the police.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The SCP-Foundation is contacted and takes custody of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe try: <em>The Foundation is alerted and SCP-XXXX is secured and contained.</em></p>
<p>Also, the Foundation is (weirdly) almost never called the 'SCP' Foundation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>LEAD RESEARCHER: Dr. Theresa Mathers, Senior Researcher</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Don't <strong>bold</strong> things after the &quot;:&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-9391 is an experienced lab assistant with a bachelors degree in biology.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Nope. The D-Class are ex-cons who were sentenced to the death row. You could specify that they acquired a degree in biology during their life prior, but they aren't considered &quot;assistants&quot;. They're just used as lab rats.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He has not been provided any information about the history of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is usually common knowledge, so you can get rid of this part.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>[&#8230;] history of SCP-XXXX. //</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Don't put a space between the text and the &quot;//&quot;, otherwise the code won't work.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He is provided with pair of safety scissors, a felt tip pen, and a bag containing 1/2 Liter (~1 pint) of blood upon entering the room.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why was he provided with these things? You should specify it in this part.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><em>Camera shows D-9391 cutting through one side of the mattress.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Put those annotations between two parentheses.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>//Dr. M responds in the negative. //</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Don't put a space between the text and the &quot;//&quot;. &gt;.&lt;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and cuts a flap</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe <em>pulls a flap</em>?</p>
<p>Also, why is he cutting the mattress? That could potentially render the anomaly unfunctional, and the Foundation wouldn't want that to happen. Remember that this is the first experiment, so they shouldn't know that the mattress can reppair itself.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Well, I never knew mattresses looked like this before you showed me that picture, but… yes it looks the same as what you showed me.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe he could only say that he didn't know that mattresses looked like that, but that it seems normal to him?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Please feel along the top and put an X on top of each of the bumps you find.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, that might damage the object. Maybe the D-Class would just count them and then see that they make some type of &quot;3&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. M seems genuinely scared and begins breathing rapidly.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What? Foundation researchers should be trained for situations like this, and should only be scared if <em>their</em> lives are at risk. Also, the D-Class might just be trying to fool her, so she shouldn't be acting like that. She might be confused, but she should prioritize asking the D-Class to explain what he's feeling.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Just kidding. It's fine doc.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>O_o That guy would be in soooooooo much trouble if he does this. The Foundation doesn't have a sense of humor when it comes to testing anomalies.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Hector</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Researchers shouldn't call the D-Class by their names unless they're being interviewed or somethin like that.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The video runs for several hours [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The &quot;[END LOG]&quot; means the video was ended. Also, didn't D-9391 had the camera? I think you could say that a security camera recorded this part.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-Foundation Ethics Department</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's the Ethics Committee.</p>
<p>Also, wait, why is she asking for approval to test the anomaly with humans? That's a common thing to do at the Foundation. Essentially that's how they all learn how the anomalies work. They should only ask for permission if they're sure something <em>truly horrible</em> is going to happen to the test subject.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I understand your hesitation to use D-9391 given his usefulness as a lab tech [&#8230;]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Researchers shouldn't have any type of emotional relationship with their test subjects. Also, if a D-Class has some type of important knowledge that might help the Foundation, then they should use another one.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Human Test 2</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Shouldn't this be 'Experiment 2'?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>EXPERIMENT # XXXX-016.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You forgot the &quot;:&quot;. Also, don't put the stop at the end.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Because he has helped with previous tests of SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What happened during those tests? Why is he reluctant to help this time? Remember that he wasn't informed of the anomaly's history, so it should be just a mattress to his eyes.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Experiment XXXX-015</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You preciously stated that it was Experiment XXXX-16.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Mathers informs D-9391 that it's time for lights out, but relents when D-9391 asks to finish his chapter.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;</p>
<p>This shouldn't be a mother-son relationship, this should be a scientific asking a lab rat to obey.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>D-9391 jumps off of SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You forgot a stop in there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>TIME: 22:16</strong><br />
After some discussion, D-9391 complies</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You didn't space these lines correctly. Also, you forgot a stop in there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Can you please tell me everything.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence should end with a &quot;?&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Mathers relents and allows D-9391 to return to his room.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this might be just my personal canon, but I think they should force him to comply. Whatever, I guess this part can stay, as the Foundation might not want to lose too many D-Class.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Foundation's Police Liaison Office.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What's that? I've never heard of that office.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>by utilizing the effects of SCP-5663 to become Suzuki B-King motorcycles</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you could only say that the D-Class escaped using another anomaly's effects, as this part doesn't seems to go along the atmosphere of the rest of the article.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>see Incident SCP-5663-I11 Report</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You can get rid of this part.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>During this escape, twenty staff including Dr. Mathers, were injured in hit and run style accidents and there were 2 fatalities.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A little rewording here: <em>During this incident, 2 staff members were killed, and 20 other were injured, including Dr. Mathers.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-XXXX was left in Lab Room 49 unsupervised</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe just <em>SCP-XXXX was left unsupervised</em>?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>staff checking the room had not been briefed on SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think at this point everyone should be aware that SCP-XXXX is anomalous. But hey should've classified it as Safe, so they might haven't worried about it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Site-11 Lab Room 49 is located in on floor 7 of Section C and is directly above Class-D dormitories.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think you can leave this just as &quot;SCP-XXXX previous containment chamber was located directly above Site-11 Class-D Dormitories.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Sy complained that floor tiles</p>
</blockquote>
<p>*that the floor tiles</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is believed that he has heard SCP-XXXX and is attempting to protect himself from it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Get rid of this sentence, it feels too expository.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It is believed this is the moment of the attack.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Get rid of this sentence too, it feels too expository.</p>
<p>Also, when did the blood got everywhere?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This video demonstrates two hitherto unknown abilities of SCP-XXXX.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this paragrahp needs some rewording. Try: <em>It has been discovered that a previously unknown creature was inhabiting SCP-XXXX. Further experimentation has shown the creature's ability to move from one mattress to another through solid surfaces, and to reach through blankets and clothing to attack its victims. This creature has been reclassified as SCP-XXXX. The original documentation is pending changes.</em></p>
<p><em>It is unclear whether SCP-XXXX targeted D-9391 specifically or if the attack was a coincidence.</em></p>
<p>Also, I'm a little confused. Why was 9391's bed labeled as SCP-XXXX-1? Are there more creatures? Does the original mattress still exhibits anomalous properties? I thought the creature was the one behind all the anomalous phenomena.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Addendum</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think this addendum is unnecessary. Are you trying to imply that the Foundation is blaming him for the murders? I think they could just amnesteciate the persons involved.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Report: Origins of SCP-XXXX</p>
</blockquote>
<p>First, the <strong>bold</strong> code doesn't work in collapsibles. Second, you should add an &quot;+&quot; and a &quot;-&quot;.</p>
<p>Third, I'm not entirely sure of this section. On one hand, I think it's unnecessary, as the only thing it does is connecting to another article. But on the other hand, I think some kind of addition like this would be interesting. It might be a good idea to get another reviewer's opinion on this section.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall, I think the Foundation you're trying to portray is too&#8230; friendly, I guess. The Foundation needs to be ice-cold.</p>
<p>Feel free to contact a member of the <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/butterfly-squad-roster">Butterfly Squad</a> to get more crit, or PM me to take another look.</p>
<p><sub>And this is my longest crit :D</sub></p>

							</div>

								
